      sources: "Sources: examples/privileged-access fixtures (fictional tenant and sign-in export). Chapter 02, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 4–7.",
      say: `Reading the findings is where the rules become real. One fictional account passes. The other fails five different ways, from the wrong domain to an expiring Global Administrator assignment. Three enforced policies don't exclude the accounts, and someone who shouldn't be there is sitting in the exclusion group. Then the offline match finds all five emergency events, where the sample found two, and ignores an ordinary user's sign-in. Finally you try to feed it a user name instead of an ID, and it refuses. Run the whole suite, and three hundred thirty tests pass.`,
      detail: `Step 4, read the findings: Get-Content out/emergency/findings.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json | Format-Table Rule, Severity, Object. ea01 passes. ea02 fails five ways: PA-EA-02 (it's on contoso.example), PA-EA-05 (its Global Administrator assignment ends on December 31), PA-EA-06 (a phone method), PA-EA-07 (not in the exclusion group) and PA-EA-09 (last validated May 1). PA-EA-08 names three enforced policies, CA001-All-Block-legacy-authentication, CA004-Admins-Require-compliant-device and PIM-Activation-c1, and reports the report-only CA090-Emergency-accounts-Report-sign-ins as Info. PA-EA-10 names carl.ops@contoso.example, who is in the exclusion group and shouldn't be. Step 5, match the same export with the example's rule: Find-PaEmergencyActivity -Rows $rows -AccountId (the two fictional object IDs) returns five rows: two from SigninLogs, one from AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs, and two from AuditLogs (Add member to role, initiated by ea02, and User registered security info, which targets ea01). Dana's ordinary sign-in isn't matched. Step 6, see the guards: Get-PaEmergencyQuery -AccountId ea01@contosoexample.onmicrosoft.com returns "'ea01@contosoexample.onmicrosoft.com' isn't an object ID"; the template's type, apiVersion and severity read Microsoft.Insights/scheduledQueryRules | 2023-12-01 | 0. Step 7, Invoke-Pester -Path tests. Expected result: 330 tests passing, and each row of the gap table seen with your own eyes. Delete out when you're done. The chapter's own tests: 7 in the Chapter 02 block of tests/MicrosoftSamples.Tests.ps1 and 29 in tests/EmergencyAccess.Tests.ps1; with Chapter 01's, 78 Pester tests pass under PowerShell 7, with no module, tenant or network.`,
